course: dictionary
lesson: 12888
title: Listing things one by one
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To enumerate means to name things one by one, like writing a list. Teachers use it in formal writing. «Please enumerate the steps of your experiment» means: list each step, in order.
explain: In maths, enumerate can mean count. «Enumerate all the possibilities» means find every possible answer and list them, so that none is missed. The list itself shows how many there are.
explain: In everyday talk, people say list or count instead. The formal word still tells you exactly what to do. When you meet it, look at the words around it: are you making a list, or finding how many?
fact: Enumerate means to name things one by one, or to count them.
fact: Enumerate is a verb. Its forms are enumerates, enumerated and enumerating, and its noun is enumeration.
fact: «Enumerate the reasons» means list the reasons one by one.
fact: In maths, «enumerate all the possibilities» means find and list every possibility, so none is missed.
fact: Everyday words for enumerate are list and count.
exercise: «Please enumerate the steps of your experiment.» What does enumerate mean here? | Forget the steps | Paint the steps red | *Name the steps one by one | Hide the steps
exercise: In maths, «enumerate all the possibilities» means: | Guess one answer | *Find and list every possibility | Draw a picture of a dog | Add up the numbers only
exercise: «She enumerated the rules before the game.» Which word is the verb in the past tense? | *enumerated | she | before | rules
test: «The shopkeeper enumerated the price of every item.» Which everyday word means the same as enumerate? | Shout | Sleep | *List | Hide
test: Which word is the noun from enumerate? | enumerating | enumerates | *enumeration | enumerate
exam: Which sentence uses enumerate correctly? | I enumerate my favourite colour is blue. | The cat will enumerate under the table. | *Please enumerate the three reasons you chose this book.
